@@s001 What the exam is like
@@s002 <span>Exam</span>
@@s003 What the exam is like
@@s004 A <b>4-hour written exam in person</b>, Monday <b>4 January 2027</b>. To get in you need <b>5 homework passed</b>. Below is everything the official documents say, and what has not yet been published.
@@s005 At a glance
@@s006 Type
@@s007 <b>Written, in person</b>, with restrictions (variant A22)
@@s008 Duration
@@s009 <b>4 hours</b>
@@s010 When
@@s011 Mon 4 Jan 2027<br>9:00–13:00
@@s012 Grade
@@s013 Danish 7-point scale, <b>external examiner</b>
@@s014 Platform
@@s015 WISEflow (login with the ITU account)
@@s016 Artificial intelligence
@@s017 <b>forbidden</b> in any form
@@s018 Admission
@@s019 5 homework passed out of 10 assessed
@@s020 Without admission
@@s021 grade <b>NA</b> and one exam attempt used up
@@s022 Date
@@s023 <code>SCHEDULE.md</code> in the repo says "4 Jan 2026": it is a typo. The official description on LearnIT says <b>Mon, 4 Jan 2027, 09:00–13:00</b>.
@@s024 What the test is like
@@s025 The precise format is explained in the lecture of week 13 ("Let's talk about the exam", 24 November) and in the <i>Examination Syllabus</i> published on LearnIT by the last lecture. What is already known from the course documents:
@@s026 <b>Small programming exercises, similar to those of the homework.</b> The course description: "most time is spent on small practical exercises resembling exam questions".
@@s027 <b>Open questions in English.</b> The week 4 sheet (exercise 9): "The exam may contain open questions to be answered in English".
@@s028 <b>The same style rules as the exercises</b>: no <code>var</code>, effects, exceptions, <code>return</code>, unless asked. See <a href="regole.html">Rules and prohibitions</a>.
@@s029 <b>Old exams</b>: the week 1 slides say "old exams will be published". Current status in <a href="prove.html">Old exams</a>.
@@s030 Exam programme
@@s031 From the official course description, with the week in which it is done:
@@s032 Topic
@@s033 Week
@@s034 Introduction to Scala 3, pure functions, recursion, higher-order functions
@@s035 S1–S2 · <a href="../teoria/s01-scala-base.html">pages</a>
@@s036 Immutable data structures, algebraic types, fold
@@s037 S2 · <a href="../teoria/s02-adt.html">pages</a>
@@s038 Partial computations, Option, monads
@@s039 S3 · <a href="../teoria/s03-option.html">pages</a>
@@s040 Call-by-name and lazy programming, streams
@@s041 S4 · <a href="../teoria/s04-valutazione.html">pages</a>
@@s042 State monad
@@s043 Property-based testing
@@s044 Parser combinators
@@s045 Monoid and Monad (functional design)
@@s046 Functional probabilistic programming
@@s047 Language semantics, lenses
@@s048 What you must be able to do (official objectives)
@@s049 Design, test and run functional programs in Scala.
@@s050 Use expressive types (polymorphism, type-level functions, higher-order types) to describe library interfaces.
@@s051 Recognise monadic structures, use monadic libraries and design them.
@@s052 Reason about eager and lazy evaluation, with advantages and disadvantages.
@@s053 Reason about APIs in pure and stateful style, comparing the designs.
@@s054 Use eager and lazy to design data structures and exploit existing lazy ones (streams).
@@s055 Implement solutions based on research papers in library and language design.
@@s056 The re-exam
@@s057 It depends on how many people sign up:
@@s058 21 or more sign-ups
@@s059 Four-hour written exam in person (A22). Internet: full access. Printed and handwritten books and notes allowed, any e-book and software, but <b>no AI</b>: no language model, no GitHub Copilot.
@@s060 20 or fewer sign-ups
@@s061 Oral with preparation: <b>30 minutes</b> of supervised preparation, then <b>20 minutes</b> of oral.
@@s062 The materials allowed are written this way only for the re-exam. For the ordinary exam the description says "with restrictions" without details: the Examination Syllabus will confirm them.
@@s063 To be confirmed
@@s064 When it comes out, I update this page
@@s065 Materials allowed at the ordinary exam (book? notes? offline computer?).
@@s066 Structure: number of questions, weight of the open questions, whether a Scala file is handed in.
